        "text": "Subsecciones de: Chapter 3: The PowerShell Pipeline \u2013 How to String Cmdlets Together\nP\u00e1ginas: 74-99\n\u2022 How to bring cmdlets together \u2013 the pipeline  (75)\n  \u2022 What is an object?  (77)\n\u2022 Selecting and sorting objects  (78)\n  \u2022 Using Select-Object  (79)\n  \u2022 Ordering objects with Sort-Object  (80)\n\u2022 Filtering objects  (83)\n  \u2022 Understanding comparison operators  (83)\n  \u2022 Understanding Where-Object advanced syntax  (85)\n  \u2022 Multiple filters in the advanced syntax  (86)\n  \u2022 Filter optimization  (89)\n\u2022 Enumerating objects  (90)\n  \u2022 Parallel enumeration  (91)\n\u2022 How the pipeline works \u2013 parameter binding  (91)\n  \u2022 Understanding ByValue parameter binding  (92)\n  \u2022 ByPropertyName parameter binding  (94)\n\u2022 Troubleshooting the pipeline \u2013 pipeline tracing  (95)\n\u2022 Summary  (97)\n\u2022 Exercises  (97)\n\u2022 Further reading  (98)\n3\nThe PowerShell Pipeline \u2013 How\nto String Cmdlets Together\nAlmost all operating systems ( OSs ) have the concept of a pipeline , allowing the output of one process\nto be fed into the input of the next. This concept is credited to Douglas McIlroy in 1973 when he was\nworking on version 3 of Unix at Bell Labs. This initial implementation envisaged the output of every\ncommand as a file-like structure, on which the next command could operate.\nThis chapter will explain how PowerShell adheres to this vision and differs from it. We\u2019ll start by\nexploring the pipeline concept, then look at some basic ways of manipulating the contents of the\npipeline before taking a medium-depth dive into how the pipeline works in PowerShell, and how we\ncan troubleshoot it when it doesn\u2019t.\nBy the end of this chapter, we\u2019ll understand how information passes from one cmdlet to the next,\nhow to manipulate that information so that we only work with the stuff we need, and how to work\nout what\u2019s going wrong when we get an error message.\nIn this chapter, we\u2019re going to cover the following topics:",
